            slug: 'protective-rites',
            title: 'Protective Rites',
            native: 'སྲུང་བ་',
            translit: 'srung ba',
            summary: 'Every Tibetan institution kept a protector, and every protector kept an oath. The rites that maintained them were the most frequently performed and least often discussed part of the entire tradition.',
            paragraphs: [
                'The protectors, chos skyong, are divided by the literature into two classes and the division is observed with care. Supramundane protectors, those beyond the world, are buddhas appearing in wrathful aspect: Mahākāla, Palden Lhamo and their kind, who may properly be taken as objects of refuge because they are not other than enlightenment wearing a terrifying face. Worldly protectors are powerful but unenlightened beings — spirits of mountains, lakes and localities, some of considerable antiquity — who are bound by oath into the service of the Dharma, offered to, propitiated and relied upon, but never taken as refuge, because a being still within saṃsāra cannot deliver anyone from it. The distinction is doctrinal, and it is also practical: it governs what may be asked of whom.',
                'The oath is the tradition\'s founding narrative. Padmasambhava, travelling to Samye in the eighth century, is said to have encountered and subjugated the indigenous deities of the land, binding each under oath to guard the teaching rather than obstruct it — dam la btags pa, \'bound to the pledge\'. Whatever the historical value of the account, and the earliest Tibetan sources give Padmasambhava a much smaller role than the later literature does, it describes with accuracy something plainly visible in the record: a Buddhist ritual system that did not abolish Tibet\'s local cults but conscripted them, keeping their names, their mountains, their lakes and a good deal of their character. Pehar, who would become the deity of Nechung and thereby of the Tibetan state, belongs squarely to this conscripted class.',
                'Mahākāla appears in forms proper to each school — Pañjaranātha for the Sakya, deriving from the Vajrapañjara Tantra; the Black-Cloaked form for the Karma Kagyu; the Six-Armed for the Gelug and the Shangpa lineage. Palden Lhamo, the pre-eminent female protector of the supramundane class though not the only one, guards Lhasa and the line of the Dalai Lamas, and is associated with Lhamo Latso, the lake in the mountains southeast of Lhasa whose surface was consulted for visions during the search for a reincarnation. The indispensable survey remains René de Nebesky-Wojkowitz\'s Oracles and Demons of Tibet, compiled in Sikkim and Kalimpong from liturgical texts and living informants and published in 1956 — a book assembled at the last moment when it could still have been assembled that way.',
                'The rites themselves are material and largely unglamorous. Torma are offered to protectors on a schedule, daily in institutions that can maintain it. Mdos are constructed ritual objects built on wooden frames, at whose centre stands the thread-cross, nam mkha\' — coloured yarn wound across crossed sticks in geometric webs, sometimes to a considerable size and complexity — which serves as a temporary residence for a summoned force, or as part of a ransom, glud, in which an effigy is offered in a person\'s place. These are dismantled and disposed of outside the settlement when the rite concludes; they are instruments, not icons, and are not kept. The literature classes the whole category as apotropaic — averting, screening, ransoming — and the commentaries attach to it the same bodhisattva constraints that govern any other tantric act; the manner of performance belongs to the transmissions that carry these rites, and no part of it is set out here. The srung \'khor is the portable form: an amulet, characteristically a woodblock-printed diagram of concentric mantra rings folded down to a small square packet, sewn into cloth and worn at the neck or carried in a ga\'u, a metal reliquary box. Examples survive in some numbers in European museum and library collections, the Wellcome Collection among them.',
                'Nechung is described here as history. The monastery of Gnas chung, beside Drepung outside Lhasa, housed the medium through whom the Tibetan government sought counsel, an office institutionalised under the Fifth Dalai Lama in the seventeenth century and continuing to the present. The office belongs not to the deity but to the medium, the sku rten or \'bodily support\', through whom Dorje Drakden, an emanation of Pehar, is held to speak. Consultations were formal occasions of state with a fixed liturgy, elaborate and very heavy ceremonial headgear, and scribes to record utterances that were characteristically oblique and required subsequent interpretation — a fact the tradition acknowledges rather than conceals. The institution moved with the Tibetan government to Dharamsala after 1959 and functions there still. Christopher Bell\'s The Dalai Lama and the Nechung Oracle (Oxford, 2021) is the current scholarly account, built on fieldwork at both the Lhasa monastery and its exile foundation, and it treats the office as what it is: a functioning religious institution with a documented history, not an exhibit.',
            ],
