            slug: 'astrology-and-medicine',
            title: 'Astrology and Medicine',
            native: 'གསོ་རིག་དང་རྩིས་',
            translit: 'gso rig dang rtsis',
            summary: 'Tibet took its calculation from two directions and its medicine from several, then spent five centuries arguing among itself about how much of the result the Buddha had actually said.',
            paragraphs: [
                'Tibetan calculation divides into dkar rtsis and nag rtsis, white and black — colours that are conventionally explained as naming countries rather than moral temperatures, and the point is regularly missed. On the standard Tibetan account, India is rgya gar and China rgya nag, the second unambiguously \'the black expanse\'; white calculation is therefore taken to mean Indian calculation and black calculation Chinese. The derivation of gar from dkar is a traditional etymology rather than a secure philological one, and a second explanation circulates that associates the white with the reckoning of the luminaries; but on either account no sinister implication attaches to either term. Dkar rtsis is the mathematical astronomy that entered Tibet with the Kālacakra Tantra: planetary longitudes, eclipse prediction, and the luni-solar calendar with its doubled and omitted days that so complicates the dating of Tibetan events. Nag rtsis is the divinatory apparatus of Chinese derivation. The sixty-year cycle, rab byung, pairing twelve animals with five elements in their yin and yang phases, is reckoned from 1027 — the year associated with the Kālacakra\'s arrival in Tibet, so that the two systems have been dating each other from the beginning.',
                'The instruments of nag rtsis are the ones a Tibetan household actually encountered. Twelve animals — mouse, ox, tiger, hare, dragon, snake, horse, sheep, monkey, bird, dog, pig — combined with wood, fire, earth, iron and water. The sme ba, nine numbers of the magic square, each carrying a colour, cycling by year, month and day. The spar kha, the eight trigrams, taken over from the Chinese bagua and given Tibetan names. From these an astrologer produced the birth horoscope, the annual reckoning, the compatibility calculation consulted before a marriage, and the death horoscope, which was consequential in the most immediate way: it determined the direction in which the body should leave the house, who might handle it, when disposal should occur and which rites were required. This was the astrologer\'s daily labour, and it touched far more lives than any doctrine in this hall.',
                'Medicine rests on the Gyüshi, the Four Tantras — Root, Explanatory, Instructional and Subsequent, one hundred and fifty-six chapters — presented as the teaching of the Medicine Buddha in the city of Tanaduk, before an assembly of gods, sages, Buddhists and non-Buddhists that neatly stages the text\'s own eclecticism. Its transmission is associated with Yuthok Yönten Gönpo the Younger in the twelfth century. The physiology turns on the three nyes pa: rlung, wind; mkhris pa, bile; and bad kan, phlegm. These are cognate with the Ayurvedic tridoṣa without being identical to them, and the Tibetan word means \'fault\', because each names a mode of imbalance rather than a substance one could point at. Diagnosis is conducted under three heads — questioning, observation and palpation — of which two techniques became celebrated. The pulse, rtsa dpyad, is taken by palpation at dawn with three fingers at each wrist, each finger assigned a pair of organs, with the assignment differing by the sex of the patient. The urine, chu dpyad, falls under observation, and is examined in a white vessel for colour, vapour, odour, bubbles, sediment and the film that forms on its surface as it cools.',
                'Tibetan physicians disputed their own foundational text for centuries, and did so in public. Zurkhar Lodrö Gyalpo (b. 1509) argued in the mid-sixteenth century, in his commentary Mes po\'i zhal lung, the Ancestors\' Advice, that the Gyüshi was a Tibetan composition rather than the word of the Buddha, pointing to its Tibetan geography and its Tibetan materia medica — an argument from internal evidence that any modern philologist would recognise, and one Yang Ga\'s study of the treatise\'s sources has since carried much further. Desi Sangye Gyatso, regent to the Fifth Dalai Lama, defended its scriptural status while conceding substantial Tibetan redaction, a compromise that allowed observation and revelation to go on sharing a room for another two centuries. Janet Gyatso\'s Being Human in a Buddhist World is the major study of that productive tension. The Desi\'s own Blue Beryl commentary of 1688 was accompanied by a set of seventy-nine painted scrolls executed under his direction between 1687 and 1703, illustrating anatomy, embryology, pulse points, surgical instruments, urine flasks, moxibustion sites and the entire pharmacopoeia — an illustrative programme without parallel in Tibet, and among the most extensive undertaken anywhere before the modern period. He also wrote the White Beryl on calculation, defended it in a further volume when it was attacked, and founded the medical college on Chagpori hill in the mid-1690s.',
            ],
